<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Mazepa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1639">1639</a>–<a href="/years/1709">1709</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Mazepa was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> from 1687 to 1708 and a generous patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> churches, learning and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv-mohyla-academy">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a> in 1708, hoping to free the Hetmanate from Russian control. After their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709 he fled to Ottoman territory, where he died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa.jpg" alt="Ivan Mazepa" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Bernigeroth (de) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iwan_Mazepa_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mazepa was born on 30 March 1639 (20 March in the old style) in the village of Mazepyntsi near Bila Tserkva, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, into a noble family. He studied at the Kyiv Mohyla Collegium and in 1659 entered the service of King John II Casimir, who sent him on diplomatic missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. From 1669 to 1673 he served under Hetman Petro Doroshenko, as a squadron commander and chancellor on missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Captured in 1674 by the Kosh Otaman Ivan Sirko, he then spent seven years as a courtier of Doroshenko's rival Ivan Samoylovych.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1687 Mazepa accused Samoylovych of conspiring to secede from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> of Left-bank Ukraine at Kolomak. By supporting the removal of the regent Sophia in 1689 he won the trust of Tsar Peter I, who valued his education. His new strategy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and their Ottoman overlords brought him the Order of St Andrew, and in 1707 Emperor Joseph I made him a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. His long rule is described as a period of recovery from the Ruin, and he patronised churches, learning and the arts.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> he supplied Peter with troops from 1700, and in 1704 the tsar allowed him to incorporate Right-bank territories into the Hetmanate. Peter's reforms steadily reduced the Hetmanate's autonomy, and in 1708, after the tsar refused to protect it against the advancing Swedes, Mazepa abandoned the alliance and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII of Sweden</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles did not allow Mazepa's forces to take part at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709. Mazepa retreated with the king to Bendery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and died there on 2 October 1709 (21 September in the old style), of a stroke following gout. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> pronounced an anathema on his name in 1708, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarchate</a> considers uncanonical. Russian authorities removed his crest from churches and blacked out his name in books. His life has inspired many literary, artistic and musical works.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Mazepa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His strategic recklessness, above all the invasion of Russia in 1708 and the decision to turn south into Ukraine in hope of support from the Cossack hetman <b>Ivan Mazepa</b>, led to the catastrophic defeat at Poltava (1709) that broke Swedish military power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter I destroyed the Sich in 1709 because its Cossacks joined <b>Mazepa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaporozhian-sich">Zaporozhian Sich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mazepintsi, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Bender, in what is now <a href="/countries/moldova">Moldova</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.91,29.98&amp;year=1684" aria-label="Mazepintsi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5237 427 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivan Mazepa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5687 697h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5687 697h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.91,29.98&amp;year=1684">What was here in 1684? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
